ugh</span> <span data-s=\"61\" data-w=\"14\">the</span> <span data-s=\"61\" data-w=\"15\">origin.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check yourself</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>For f(x) = 0.2 x + 30, what is the prediction at x = 100, and what does 30 mean?</li>\n<li>Why do we square the errors instead of just adding them?</li>\n<li>Why can we not trust a fitted line far outside the data it saw?</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answers\"><summary>Show answers</summary><p> (1) 0.2 x 100 + 30 = 50; 30 is the prediction at x = 0, the bias. (2) Plain errors above and below the line cancel each other, and squaring also punishes big misses. (3) The relationship may change outside that range, and the data has no evidence about it.</p></details>\n<p><strong>Go deeper:</strong> Ng, course 1, videos 9 and 10 · the scikit-learn <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/linear_model.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">linear models guide</a> · <em>An Introduction to Statistical Learning</em>, chapter 3 (free book, <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">statlearning.com</a>).</p>\n"}